## Proof

**Answer: Yes, $\dim(R) = 0$.**

**Key observation.** For every prime ideal $\mathfrak{p} \subset R$, the complement $S_{\mathfrak{p}} := R \setminus \mathfrak{p}$ is a multiplicative subset of $R$ (since $\mathfrak{p}$ is prime, $R\setminus\mathfrak{p}$ is closed under multiplication and contains $1$). The corresponding localization is the local ring $R_{\mathfrak{p}}$.

By hypothesis, $\dim(S^{-1}R) = 0$ for **every** multiplicative subset $S$, so in particular

$$\dim(R_{\mathfrak{p}}) = 0 \quad \text{for every prime } \mathfrak{p} \subset R. \tag{1}$$

**Standard fact.** The prime ideals of $R_{\mathfrak{p}}$ are in order-preserving bijection with the prime ideals of $R$ contained in $\mathfrak{p}$ (via $\mathfrak{q} \mapsto S_{\mathfrak{p}}^{-1}\mathfrak{q}$). Consequently, chains of primes in $R_{\mathfrak{p}}$ correspond exactly to chains of primes in $R$ terminating at $\mathfrak{p}$, and therefore

$$\dim(R_{\mathfrak{p}}) = \operatorname{ht}(\mathfrak{p}),$$

where $\operatorname{ht}(\mathfrak{p})$ is the height of $\mathfrak{p}$, i.e. the supremum of lengths of chains $\mathfrak{q}_0 \subsetneq \mathfrak{q}_1 \subsetneq \cdots \subsetneq \mathfrak{q}_n = \mathfrak{p}$.

Combining with (1):

$$\operatorname{ht}(\mathfrak{p}) = 0 \quad \text{for every prime } \mathfrak{p} \subset R. \tag{2}$$

**Conclusion.** Suppose, for contradiction, that $\dim(R) \geq 1$. Then there exist prime ideals $\mathfrak{q} \subsetneq \mathfrak{p}$ in $R$. Taking this $\mathfrak{p}$, we have a chain of length $\geq 1$ ending at $\mathfrak{p}$, so $\operatorname{ht}(\mathfrak{p}) \geq 1$, contradicting (2).

Hence no strict inclusion between prime ideals of $R$ exists. The longest chain of primes has length $0$ (a single prime), so

$$\dim(R) = 0. \qquad \blacksquare$$

